course: dictionary
lesson: 7975
title: Recognition
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Recognition is a noun. It means knowing someone or something because you have seen or heard it before. «Face recognition looks for patterns in faces» means a computer can spot a face it has seen before, and a person can do the same thing when they know a friend across a busy playground.
explain: Recognition can also mean praise or thanks for something good that someone has done. Schools give certificates and awards for recognition of hard work. Here recognition means "we noticed what you did, and we thank you for it."
explain: Recognition can mean accepting that something is true or real. Context clues help: if a text says «Her recognition that she had made a mistake took courage», the word tells you she accepted the truth, even though it was hard. Look at the words around recognition to find out which meaning fits.
fact: Recognition means knowing something because you have met it before.
fact: Recognition can mean praise or thanks, as in an award given «in recognition of hard work».
fact: Recognition can mean accepting that something is true or real.
fact: «Face recognition» is a computer identifying faces it has seen before.
fact: «Very similar faces, like identical twins, can sometimes fool some systems.» So recognition systems are not perfect.
fact: Phrases to learn: «in recognition of», «beyond recognition» (so changed you cannot tell what it is), and «gain recognition» (become known or respected).
exercise: «Face recognition looks for patterns in faces.» What does face recognition do? | Draws pictures of faces | Gives a prize to the best face | *Identifies a face it has seen before | Hides faces from view
exercise: The school gave her a medal in recognition of her hard work. What does recognition mean here? | *Praise or thanks for something good | Knowing her face from before | A mistake in the record | A test she must take
exercise: «Her recognition that she had made a mistake took courage.» What does recognition mean? | *Accepting that something is true | Forgetting something | Measuring the length of something | Hiding a secret
test: «Very similar faces, like identical twins, can sometimes fool face recognition.» Why might the computer get it wrong? | The computer is asleep | *The twins look very alike, so the system can be fooled | The faces are too bright | Twins never have faces
test: The old house was damaged by the storm, and it was now beyond recognition. What does beyond recognition mean? | *So changed you can no longer tell what it is | Painted bright blue | Sold to a new owner | Easy to recognise from far away
exam: Which sentence uses recognition to mean praise? | Her face showed no sign of recognition at all, and she walked past her old friend | The computer scanned the faces of the crowd | The bus was late, so we waited in the rain | *The mayor spoke of the nurses' hard work in recognition of their care
